        <p>Getting a bottle delivered in Austin used to mean squinting at a third-party app, hoping the store on the other end had what you picked. Ordering straight from a local liquor store is simpler than that, and once you've done it, it's hard to go back. Here's how it works at Monarch, start to finish.</p>

        <h2>Local Store vs. App Marketplace: What's the Difference?</h2>
        <p>Big delivery apps are marketplaces. They list a menu, route your order to whichever store is nearby, and put a driver and a support chatbot between you and the person actually pulling your bottle. When something's out of stock or your building has a tricky gate, you're talking to a call center.</p>
        <p>Ordering from a local store cuts the middle out:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>We pack from our own shelf.</strong> What you see online is what's in our stores. If we're low on something, we know before your order goes out.</li>
          <li><strong>One phone number.</strong> <a href="tel:5126144949">(512) 614-4949</a> reaches a person standing in the store, not a queue.</li>
          <li><strong>Local staff who know the bottles.</strong> Want a swap suggestion for a sold-out rye? The person packing your order has probably tasted the alternative.</li>
          <li><strong>Your dollars stay in the neighborhood.</strong> Monarch has been family-owned (father and son) since we opened in 2020.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Step by Step: How to Order</h2>
        <h3>1. Shop online</h3>
        <p>Browse our online store from your phone or laptop. It's organized by category, so you can jump straight to bourbon, tequila, wine, or the beer and seltzer section, and filter for Texas brands if you're feeling local. Not sure what to get? Our <a href="/guides/best-bourbon-whiskey-austin.html">bourbon guide</a> and <a href="/guides/classic-cocktails-at-home.html">cocktails guide</a> are good starting points.</p>
        <h3>2. Choose delivery or curbside pickup</h3>
        <p>At checkout you'll pick how you want it. Delivery brings the order to your address. Curbside means you drive to <a href="/east-austin.html">East Austin</a> or <a href="/lakeway.html">Lakeway</a>, park out front, and call when you arrive; we walk it out to the car. Delivery coverage and terms live on our <a href="/delivery.html">delivery page</a>, and they're worth a quick read before you order.</p>
        <h3>3. ID at the door (or the car window)</h3>
        <p>This is the non-negotiable part. Texas law requires that alcohol delivery be made only to someone 21 or older who shows a valid government-issued photo ID. Our driver checks it at handoff every time, even if we recognize you. For curbside, we check the ID of the person picking up at the car.</p>

        <h2>What to Have Ready</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Your ID.</strong> Driver's license, state ID, passport, or military ID. Expired doesn't count.</li>
          <li><strong>A reachable phone.</strong> The driver may call or text if the gate code, apartment number, or parking situation needs a hand.</li>
          <li><strong>Someone home who is 21+.</strong> We can't leave alcohol unattended: no porch drops, no "leave it with the front desk," no handing it to a roommate under 21. If the recipient appears intoxicated, the driver must decline the handoff.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If no one eligible is available, the order returns to the store. It's a pain for everyone, so a quick check that you'll be home is the best thing you can do for a smooth delivery.</p>

        <h2>Sending a Gift</h2>
        <p>Delivery makes a great gift move for a birthday, a housewarming, or a "sorry I missed the party." A few things to know:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The recipient must be 21 or older and show valid ID, just like any delivery. Give them a heads-up so they're not surprised at the door.</li>
          <li>Use the order notes for a short message; we'll pass it along.</li>
          <li>Champagne, a good bourbon, or a Texas whiskey from our <a href="/guides/texas-spirits-guide.html">Texas spirits guide</a> are our most-requested gift bottles.</li>
        </ul>

